class Solution {
    public int removeElement(int[] nums, int val) {
        
        // j is the index where the next valid element (not equal to val) will be placed
        int j = 0;

        // Traverse the entire array using i
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){

            // Check if the current element is NOT equal to the value we want to remove
            if(nums[i] != val){

               // If it is not equal, copy the element to index j
               // This shifts valid elements toward the beginning of the array
               nums[j] = nums[i];

               // Move j to the next position for the next valid element
               j++;
            }
        }

        // j represents the count of elements that are not equal to val
        // This is the new length of the modified array
        return j;
    }
}

Time Complexity:

O(n) → you traverse the array only once.

Space Complexity:

O(1) → no extra memory used (in-place modification).
